cpp — V7's C preprocessor, host-ported
=====================================

This is John Reiser's 1978 preprocessor (V7 `/usr/src/cmd/cpp`), built to run
on a modern 64-bit host.  It emits the same preprocessed C text the PDP-11
version did, so it can front a cross-compiling `cc`.

Sources
-------

V7 ships cpp as three files:

  cpp.c     the preprocessor proper (macro expansion, #include, #define, ...)
  cpy.y     a yacc grammar for `#if` expressions
  yylex.c   the `#if` lexer, `#include`'d into the generated cpy.c

Host-port changes (relative to c99/)

  * cpy.y: V7-yacc wrote actions as `={ ... }`; the `=` is stripped so
    bison -y accepts it, and a `%{ %}` prologue declares `yylex`/`yyerror`.
  * cpp.c: `pperror`/`ppwarn` were K&R "generic argument" functions (a format
    string plus two args that could be an int *or* a pointer, both 16-bit on
    the PDP-11).  Rebuilt as real varargs (`vfprintf`).  `fout` is assigned in
    `main()` (glibc's `stdout` is a runtime value), and the `_sobuf` stdio
    buffer is a local static.
  * Forward declarations of `lookup`/`slookup`/`strdex` were empty `()`; made
    into proper prototypes (the empty `()` collides with `short`-typed params).

Build and test
--------------

    make            # builds ./cpp
    make check      # golden test: tests/test.i -> tests/test.expected

The `-P` flag suppresses the `# line "file"` markers (the `# 12 "foo.c"` lines
V7's cpp emits to preserve line numbers).
